Bash  cat > /tmp/final_genEmit <<'XEOF'
/*****************************************************************************
    genEmit -- genParse's emitter side, IN KANT.

    This is where converted methods land. `emitLeaf` is the first one: register a
    `spellLeaf` here and the C++ `emitLeaf` (genParse.rtn) routes to it instead
    of running its own body; register nothing and the C++ body runs unchanged.

    THE INTERFACE, which is the C++ fork's side of the seam (genParse.rtn,
    `locateSpeller`/`spellKant`):

        registry            Spellers      -- and only a registry of that name
        action              spellLeaf     -- and only under that name
        argument            the PLAN NODE  (tag is the kind: LIT LITTO CALL
                                            MANY OPT)
        argument.sink       an attribute, "label" or "into" -- the FOLD's
                            decision, so it is the one thing genuinely external
                            to the node
        returns             a field whose content is the spelling

    `local` is NOT passed. It derives from the node's own `at` attribute, which
    every node carries INCLUDING an OPT's wrapped inner node -- planTerm builds
    `at` before the OPT wrap, so both end up with the same index.

    WHEN A SPELLER IS REGISTERED ITS ANSWER IS AUTHORITATIVE, INCLUDING NULL.
    There is no fallback on a null result: falling back would mean a kant bug
    silently produced the right text, and the handover is only worth anything if
    a kant defect is VISIBLE.

    THE ORACLE IS `genLadder/spell.target`, captured from the C++ emitLeaf while
    it was still the only implementation. All six plan kinds and the refusal
    path, both sinks on every node.

    -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    THREE THINGS ABOUT THIS BODY THAT ARE NOT OPTIONAL. Each cost a run to find
    and each is written up in docs/kantCorpus.md (CLAIM KANT-6/7/8/9).

    1. RECURSION IS SPELLED `this(...)`, NOT `spellLeaf(...)`. The self-name
       spelling that walk/printDefinition/toXML use does NOT work through this
       seam: the first invocation dies with

           GroupItem add: Tried to add spellLeaf to itself      EXIT=139

       aCTionNamE resolves the bare name to the action and then adds it to
       itself. `this` is the local every coded field gets at define time
       (ruleActions.rtn, NewGroup += "this"), is flagged isLocal, and so takes
       none of that path.

    2. NOTHING MAY READ A LOCAL AFTER THE RECURSIVE CALL except the call's own
       result. `this(...)` does not trip the field.recursive inference (that test
       is a syntactic identity check against currentMETHOD), so locals are NOT
       per-frame here: probing `kind` after the call read CALL/LIT, the INNER
       frame's value, not OPT. `piece` is safe only because the inner frame never
       assigns it -- planTerm cannot nest an OPT inside an OPT. Do not add a
       post-call read without re-checking that.

    3. THE ITERATOR IS A HANDLE; `wrapped <- inner` IS WHAT DEREFERENCES IT.
       `inner.taG` answers "inner", not "CALL" -- runOP exempts an iterator in
       TARGET position from the unwrap, so `inner :% sink` would hang the sink
       attribute on the ITERATOR. In ARGUMENT position it does deref, which is
       what `<-` exploits: `wrapped` ends up pointing at the real plan node, and
       `wrapped :% sink` then lands on the node.

    KNOWN DIVERGENCES FROM THE C++, both on the refusal path, which no target
    covers -- recorded, not papered over (BLOCKED KANT-B1/B2):
      - the C++ diagnostics go to stderr (cerr); incant `print` reaches stdout
        or a buffer and there is no stderr spelling, so these lines land on the
        other stream.
      - the C++ returns NULL to refuse. No kant spelling produces a null across
        runAction: `return;` yields "return", `return null;` yields "0",
        `return <unset local>;` yields the local's tag, `leaf = "";` yields
        "quoteBody". So a kant refusal is LOUD but not REFUSING -- emitPlan would
        take the junk text as a spelling.
*****************************************************************************/
registry(Spellers);
define
    spellLeaf argument code={
        :argument at sink slot site;
        local = string $"t" at;
        kind = argument.taG;
        if kind == "OPT";
            iterate inner on argument members;
            if ++inner;
                wrapped <- inner;
                wrapped :% sink;
                piece = this(wrapped);
                leaf = string $"(" piece _ "|| 1)";
            else
                print "emitLeaf: OPT node has no wrapped term":;
        or kind == "MANY";
            leaf = string $"many" site "(" sink "," local ")";
        or kind == "CALL";
            leaf = string $"parseR(" local "," sink ")";
        or kind == "LIT";
            leaf = string $"lit(" local "," '"' argument '"' ")";
        or kind == "LITTO";
            if sink == "into";
                leaf = string $"litOption(" local ",into," '"' argument '"' ")";
            else
                leaf = string $"litTo(" local ",label," '"' argument '"' "," '"' slot '"' ")";
        else
            print "emitLeaf: no emission for plan kind" kind:;
        return leaf;
        };
    ;
XEOF
